c":"Algerian politician and diplomat","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2019_Comprehensive_Test-Ban_Treaty_Article_XIV_Conference_(48832049572).jpg/330px-2019_Comprehensive_Test-Ban_Treaty_Article_XIV_Conference_(48832049572).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1958-09-01","died":null,"birthplace":"Constantine","parties":["independent politician"],"education":["National School of Administration"],"occupations":["diplomat","politician"],"wiki":"Sabri Boukadoum"},"Q80100650":{"id":"Q80100650","name":"Abd al-Aziz Jarad","desc":"Prime Minister of Algeria from 2019 to 2021","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Abdelaziz_Djerad_(cropped).jpg/330px-Abdelaziz_Djerad_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1954-02-12","died":null,"birthplace":"Khenchela","parties":["National Liberation Front"],"education":["University of Algiers 1","Paris Nanterre University"],"occupations":["politician","university teacher","political scientist"],"wiki":"Abdelaziz Djerad"},"Q96612848":{"id":"Q96612848","name":"Ayman ibn Abd ar-Rahman","desc":"Algerian politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Aymen-Benabderrahmane-2021_(cropped).png/330px-Aymen-Benabderrahmane-2021_(cropped).png","gender":"M","born":"1966-08-30","died":null,"birthplace":"Algiers","parties":["independent politician"],"education":["National School of Administration"],"occupations":["politician","economist"],"wiki":"Aymen Benabderrahmane"},"Q109784588":{"id":"Q109784588","name":"Nadir al-Arbawi","desc":"Prime Minister of the People's Democratic Republic of Algeria","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Nadir_Larbaoui_2023.jpg/330px-Nadir_Larbaoui_2023.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1949-09-26","died":null,"birthplace":"Tébessa","parties":[],"education":["University of Algiers 1"],"occupations":["politician","diplomat","handball player"],"wiki":"Nadir Larbaoui"},"Q133540883":{"id":"Q133540883","name":"Sifi Ghrieb","desc":"Algerian politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Sifi_Ghrieb_Moscow_2026.jpg/330px-Sifi_Ghrieb_Moscow_2026.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1971-01-20","died":null,"birthplace":null,"parties":[],"education":["Badji Mokhtar-Annaba University"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Sifi Ghrieb"}},"summary":"Algeria, officially the People's Democratic Republic of Algeria, is a country in the Maghreb region of North Africa. It is bordered to the northeast by Tunisia; to the east by Libya; to the southeast by Niger; to the southwest by Mali, Mauritania, and Western Sahara; to the west by Morocco; and to the north by the Mediterranean Sea. Its capital and largest city is Algiers.\nAlgeria was shaped first by indigenous Amazigh societies, including the Numidians, whose kingdom became one of the earliest major states in the region, as well as by Phoenician, Roman, Vandal, and Byzantine influences. From the 7th century onward, the spread of Islam and  arrival of Arab tribes gradually transformed the region, contributing to the development of a distinct Algerian identity rooted in both Arab, Islamic and Amazigh heritage. Between the 8th and 15th centuries, Algeria was ruled by a succession of local Islamic dynasties. In 1516, the Regency of Algiers was established, gradually emerging as a largely autonomous state owing nominal allegiance to the Ottoman Empire. After nearly three centuries as a major power in the Mediterranean, the country was invaded by France in 1830 and formally annexed in 1848, though it was not fully conquered and pacified until 1903. French rule brought mass European settlement that displaced the local population; by mid-1870, indigenous Algerians declined by up to a third due to warfare, disease, and starvation. French colonization faced continuous Algerian resistance, from Emir Abdelkader's campaigns and the Mokrani Revolt to the rise of nationalist movements in the 20th century. The Sétif and Guelma massacre in 1945 catalysed local resistance that culminated in the outbreak of the Algerian War in 1954. Algeria gained independence in 1962. It descended into a bloody civil war from 1992 to 2002, remaining in an official state of emergency until the 2010–2012 Algerian protests during the Arab Spring.\nAlgeria is a semi-arid country spanning over 2,381,741 square kilometres (919,595 sq mi) with a population of 47 million, making it the tenth largest in the world and the tenth-most populous country in Africa. The Sahara covers most of Algeria except for the fertile and mountainous north, where the population is concentrated. Its official languages are Arabic and Amazigh, while the vast majority of the population speak Algerian Arabic. French is also influential, although it has no official status and is gradually being replaced by English as the primary foreign language. Most Algerians identify as Arabs, while Berbers form a sizeable minority. Sunni Islam is the official religion and practiced by 99% of the population.\nAlgeria is a semi-presidential republic. Algeria is a member of various regional and international organizations. It is a regional power in North Africa and a middle power in global affairs, with the largest military budget in Africa. Algeria has the highest Human Development Index in continental Africa, and the third largest economy in Africa. Its petroleum and natural gas reserves are globally the sixteenth and ninth largest respectively.\n\n","history":"Algeria's history has been shaped by its position between the Mediterranean, the Maghreb, and the Sahara. While much of its political life developed along the northern coastal plains, the region's deeper foundations lie in indigenous Amazigh societies, including the Numidians, who formed one of the earliest known states in the area. Over time, Algeria came into contact with Phoenician, Roman, Vandal, and Byzantine powers, each leaving traces on its historical landscape. The spread of Islam from the 7th century, followed by Arab migrations, gradually reshaped the region's culture, language, and religious identity, producing an Algerian identity rooted in both Arab-Islamic and Amazigh heritage. After the weakening of Umayyad authority following the Berber Revolt of 740,  Algeria was ruled by several local Islamic dynasties, including the Rustamids, Fatimids, Zirids, Hammadids, Almohads, and Zayyanids. In the Ottoman era, the Regency of Algiers rose as a powerful and largely self-governing state in the western Mediterranean, maintaining only nominal allegiance to the Ottoman Empire. France invaded Algeria in 1830, annexed it in 1848, and extended control over the territory only after decades of resistance. In modern times, Algeria's history has been especially marked by the War of Independence and the Civil War of the 1990s.","historyTitle":"History of Algeria","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,11424900],[1961,11628900],[1962,11800800],[1963,11982100],[1964,12179800],[1965,12366000],[1966,12590100],[1967,12876100],[1968,13172600],[1969,13476000],[1970,13783700],[1971,14098900],[1972,14427100],[1973,14771100],[1974,15133500],[1975,15678600],[1976,16409200],[1977,17016000],[1978,17507000],[1979,18036700],[1980,18607200],[1981,19220700],[1982,19872300],[1983,20558100],[1984,21272000],[1985,22008500],[1986,22745500],[1987,23443600],[1988,24109500],[1989,24754500],[1990,25375800],[1991,25987900],[1992,26628600],[1993,27277000],[1994,27887300],[1995,28470200],[1996,29033000],[1997,29579300],[1998,30054100],[1999,30474400],[2000,30903900],[2001,31331200],[2002,31750800],[2003,32175800],[2004,32628300],[2005,33109200],[2006,33623500],[2007,34189400],[2008,34817000],[2009,35490400],[2010,36188200],[2011,36903400],[2012,37646200],[2013,38414200],[2014,39205000],[2015,40019500],[2016,40850700],[2017,41689300],[2018,42505000],[2019,43294500],[2020,44042100],[2021,44761100],[2022,45477400],[2023,46164200],[2024,46814300],[2025,47435300]],"gdpPerCapita":[[1960,238.392],[1961,209.371],[196